    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I'm studying for my circuits final and I'm a bit stuck on capacitors. Can you explain how they behave differently when they're in an AC circuit vs a DC circuit? Also, what's the formula for capacitive reactance? thanks.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Certainly! In a DC circuit, capacitors eventually allow the charge to flow through once they are fully charged, behaving as an open circuit. However, in an AC circuit, capacitors continuously charge and discharge as the voltage changes polarity, effectively allowing the AC current to pass through. As a result, capacitors in AC circuits resist zero-crossing points but allow the current to flow smoothly, making them useful in tuning circuits. The formula for capacitive reactance (Xc) is Xc = 1 / (2πfC), where f is the frequency of the AC signal and C is the capacitance.",
