    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "ok, so it's all about energy storage and the timing/phase shifting. can you go a bit deeper into what happens at extremely high frequencies? like, what happens to the reactance as the frequency gets closer and closer to infinity? does it just disappear?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Sure thing. Capacitive reactance indeed becomes smaller as the frequency of the AC signal increases, meaning capacitors present less opposition to AC currents at high frequencies. When the frequency approaches infinity, the formula for capacitive reactance, Xc = 1 / (2πfC), yields a result approaching zero. At infinite frequency, the capacitor wouldn't have time to fully charge any longer, and its capacity to store energy essentially fails. This is why in extremely high-frequency AC circuits, capacitors are often considered to have negligible reactance and behave almost like short circuits. This behavior is used in applications where very low impedance at high frequencies is desired, but it is also why they're not ideal for very high-frequency AC applications, as their ability to block or react to AC purchases is virtually diminished.",
